==English==

===Etymology===
Probably of {{uder|en|gmq|-}} origin: compare {{cog|da|slumpe||to stumble upon by chance}}, {{cog|no|slumpe||happen by chance}}, {{cog|sv|slumpa||to sell off}}. Compare also {{cog|de|schlumpen||to trail; draggle; be sloppy}}, dialectal {{cog|nl|slompen|t=to walk clumsily}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/slʌmp/}}
* {{audio|en|En-au-slump.ogg|a=AU}}
* {{rhymes|en|ʌmp|s=1}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[collapse]] [[heavily]] or [[helplessly]].
#: {{ux|en|Exhausted, he '''slumped''' down onto the sofa.}}
#* {{RQ:Chambers Younger Set|IX|passage=“Heavens!” exclaimed Nina, “the blue-stocking and the fogy!—and yours ''are'' pale blue, Eileen!—you’re about as self-conscious as Drina—'''slumping''' there with your hair tumbling ''à la'' Mérode! Oh, it's very picturesque, of course, but a straight spine and good grooming is better.{{nb...}}”}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[decline]] or [[fall off]] in [[activity]] or [[performance]].
#: {{ux|en|Real estate prices '''slumped''' during the recession.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=October 29, 2011|author=Phil McNulty|work=BBC Sport
|title=[http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/15413593.stm Chelsea 3-5 Arsenal]
|passage=The Gunners captain demonstrated his importance to the team by taking his tally to an outstanding 28 goals in 27 Premier League games as Chelsea '''slumped''' again after their shock defeat at QPR last week.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2021 December 29|author=Nigel Harris|title=Comment: Problems galore in 2021...|journal=RAIL|issue=947|page=3|text=But in the week ending December 6, usage '''slumped''' from 72% of pre-pandemic numbers to just 56%, following revised advice that we should work from home again.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[slouch]] or [[droop]].
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[lump]]; to throw together messily.
#* {{RQ:Hamilton Metaphysics and Logic
|passage=These different groups{{...}}are exclusively '''slumped''' together under that sense.}}
# To fall or sink suddenly through or in, when walking on a surface, as on thawing snow or ice, a bog, etc.
#* {{RQ:Barrow Works|sermonname=The Danger and Mischief of Delaying Repentance|passage=The latter walk on a bottomless quag, into which unawares they may '''slump'''.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|slang}} To cause to collapse; to hit hard; to render unconscious; to kill.

